WEBVTT

1
00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:15.420
The option Greeks course is

2
00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:17.730
designed to familiarize
traders with a set of

3
00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:22.080
risk factors used to monitor
a portfolio's profile,

4
00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.510
known as the Greeks.

5
00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:26.510
In this lesson you will learn why

6
00:00:26.510 --> 00:00:28.880
some option prices
are more or less

7
00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:31.160
responsive to changing prices

8
00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:34.495
in the value of the
underlying security.

9
00:00:34.495 --> 00:00:37.250
In this lesson we will
explain the definition of

10
00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:39.530
each of the Greek
variables and explain to

11
00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:42.680
you using an interactive
options calculator

12
00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:45.545
what influences days
to exploration,

13
00:00:45.545 --> 00:00:47.000
interest rates and the

14
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:49.625
proximity between the
option strike price

15
00:00:49.625 --> 00:00:51.815
and the price of
the underlying have

16
00:00:51.815 --> 00:00:54.845
on the price of an
option over time.

17
00:00:54.845 --> 00:00:57.230
When option traders
understand what

18
00:00:57.230 --> 00:00:59.810
basic inputs determine
the pricing model,

19
00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:01.400
they are ready to move
into dealing with

20
00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:04.940
option portfolio's risk
measures or Greeks.

21
00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:08.480
These risk variables are
called the Greeks because,

22
00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:09.995
with the exception of Vega,

23
00:01:09.995 --> 00:01:12.110
each risk measure is represented

24
00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:14.930
by a letter of the
Greek alphabet.

25
00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:17.060
The Greeks are dependent upon

26
00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:20.375
the inputs for an
options pricing model.

27
00:01:20.375 --> 00:01:23.600
This lesson assumes that
the viewer is familiar with

28
00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:27.455
basic option risks,
strategies and terminology.

29
00:01:27.455 --> 00:01:29.915
As discussed in prior lessons,

30
00:01:29.915 --> 00:01:31.280
options trading provides

31
00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:33.499
additional leverage
to trading equities,

32
00:01:33.499 --> 00:01:36.380
and thus carries increased risk.

33
00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:38.450
Options involve risk and are

34
00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:40.535
not suitable for all investors.

35
00:01:40.535 --> 00:01:42.380
For more information, read

36
00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:46.685
the Characteristics and Risks
of Standardized Options.

37
00:01:46.685 --> 00:01:53.400
For a copy, call 312-542-6901.

38
00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:56.500
An option buyer can loose
the entire premium.

39
00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:00.560
The option seller may have
unlimited risk of loss.

40
00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:04.480
This lesson incorporates an
online options calculator

41
00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:07.130
from the options
Industry Council.

42
00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:09.199
It's a free, interactive

43
00:02:09.199 --> 00:02:13.450
resource available from
optionseducation.org.

44
00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:16.610
Throughout this lesson,
we explain why each

45
00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:19.220
variable impacts the
price of an option,

46
00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:23.030
and we use the calculator to
illustrate those changes.

47
00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:26.915
Access the calculator to
type the inputs on screen

48
00:02:26.915 --> 00:02:28.670
and note the change to call and

49
00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:31.385
put prices as you vary them.

50
00:02:31.385 --> 00:02:34.550
Delta represents the
theoretical change in

51
00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:35.690
an option's price for

52
00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:39.220
a $1 change in the price
of the underlying asset.

53
00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:40.905
For a call option,

54
00:02:40.905 --> 00:02:43.810
Delta will range
from zero to one.

55
00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:47.000
As the price of the
underlying asset increases

56
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:49.825
beyond the strike price and
gets further in the money,

57
00:02:49.825 --> 00:02:52.580
the Delta will increase
and approach one,

58
00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:55.310
meaning that there is a
greater change to the price of

59
00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:57.170
the option relative to the change

60
00:02:57.170 --> 00:02:59.665
in price of the
underlying assets.

61
00:02:59.665 --> 00:03:02.400
An at-the-money-option,
Germany has

62
00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:05.655
a Delta of approximately 0.5.

63
00:03:05.655 --> 00:03:08.930
Alternatively, Delta
can be thought of as

64
00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:13.420
the probability that an option
will finish in the money.

65
00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:15.375
For a put option,

66
00:03:15.375 --> 00:03:18.635
Delta will range from
negative one to zero.

67
00:03:18.635 --> 00:03:21.710
As the price of the
underlying asset decreases

68
00:03:21.710 --> 00:03:24.800
below the strike price and
gets further in the money,

69
00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:28.370
the Delta will decrease
and approach negative one,

70
00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:30.290
meaning that there
is a greater change

71
00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:31.700
to the price of the option

72
00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:33.230
relative to the change in

73
00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:35.870
the price of the
underlying assets.

74
00:03:35.870 --> 00:03:41.080
An at-the-money-option will
have a Delta of negative 0.5.

75
00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:45.470
Alternatively, the Delta's
absolute value can also be

76
00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:47.420
thought of as the
probability that

77
00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:50.150
an option will land in the money.

78
00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:53.720
As an example, if the
underlying asset price were to

79
00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:59.180
rise from $35.90 to $36.90,

80
00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:00.805
a $1 dollar increase,

81
00:04:00.805 --> 00:04:02.840
the average Delta would

82
00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:05.930
approximate the change
in the option price.

83
00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:10.125
Underlying price, strike
price, interest rate,

84
00:04:10.125 --> 00:04:12.270
volatility and days to

85
00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:16.115
expiration inputs will have
an impact on the Delta.

86
00:04:16.115 --> 00:04:18.380
You can change the
model inputs in

87
00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:20.410
the options calculator and click

88
00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:22.070
calculate to better understand

89
00:04:22.070 --> 00:04:25.550
the impact of the model
inputs on the Delta.

90
00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:28.670
Gamma represents the
rate of change of

91
00:04:28.670 --> 00:04:32.570
Delta for a given change in
the underlying asset's price.

92
00:04:32.570 --> 00:04:36.350
Gamma is largest at
the money and becomes

93
00:04:36.350 --> 00:04:41.165
smaller as options move further
into or out of the money.

94
00:04:41.165 --> 00:04:45.305
The change in an option's
price before expiration

95
00:04:45.305 --> 00:04:49.640
is represented by a curved
line and not a flat line.

96
00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:52.370
The Delta represents the change

97
00:04:52.370 --> 00:04:54.680
at any given point
on the curved line,

98
00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:56.930
and the Gamma describes
the change in

99
00:04:56.930 --> 00:04:59.945
the Delta or curvature
in the line.

100
00:04:59.945 --> 00:05:04.400
For calculus fans, it is the
second order derivative.

101
00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:08.090
Understanding the gamma in
a portfolio is critical for

102
00:05:08.090 --> 00:05:09.470
a trader that is trying to

103
00:05:09.470 --> 00:05:12.005
continuously hedge a portfolio.

104
00:05:12.005 --> 00:05:15.320
As an example, if the
underlying asset price which

105
00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:19.370
arise from $35.90 to $36.91,

106
00:05:19.370 --> 00:05:22.700
a one dollar move,
the old Delta plus

107
00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:26.900
the average Gamma would
approximate the new delta.

108
00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:28.880
Change the model inputs on

109
00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:31.429
the option calculator
and click Calculate

110
00:05:31.429 --> 00:05:33.350
to better understand
the impact of

111
00:05:33.350 --> 00:05:36.290
the model inputs on the Gamma.

112
00:05:36.290 --> 00:05:38.930
Theta describes the relationship

113
00:05:38.930 --> 00:05:41.675
between time and the
price of an option.

114
00:05:41.675 --> 00:05:45.995
Theta only impacts the
extrinsic value of an option,

115
00:05:45.995 --> 00:05:49.520
that's because intrinsic
value does not erode,

116
00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:51.170
it only moves with a change

117
00:05:51.170 --> 00:05:53.330
in the underlying asset price.

118
00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:55.685
As an option nears expiration,

119
00:05:55.685 --> 00:05:58.625
Theta will become
increasingly negative.

120
00:05:58.625 --> 00:06:01.415
The closer an option
is to exploration,

121
00:06:01.415 --> 00:06:05.765
the less time value,
extrinsic value, remains.

122
00:06:05.765 --> 00:06:08.600
Theta measures the
approximate decline in

123
00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:12.365
an options premium due
to the passage of time.

124
00:06:12.365 --> 00:06:15.230
In other words, it measures
that rate at which

125
00:06:15.230 --> 00:06:18.105
an option's extrinsic
value is declining.

126
00:06:18.105 --> 00:06:21.325
Change the options
calculator model inputs

127
00:06:21.325 --> 00:06:22.540
and click Calculate

128
00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:24.460
to better understand
the impact of

129
00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:27.465
the model inputs on the Theta.

130
00:06:27.465 --> 00:06:29.870
Vega represents the change in

131
00:06:29.870 --> 00:06:31.130
the option's price due to

132
00:06:31.130 --> 00:06:33.785
a change in its
implied volatility.

133
00:06:33.785 --> 00:06:39.380
Vega is always greatest at the
money option and decreases

134
00:06:39.380 --> 00:06:41.585
as the underlying
asset price moves

135
00:06:41.585 --> 00:06:45.560
increasingly into the
money or out of the money.

136
00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:49.280
An increase in volatility
of one percent will change

137
00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:50.510
the options price by

138
00:06:50.510 --> 00:06:53.405
approximately the
average of the vega.

139
00:06:53.405 --> 00:06:56.750
Change the model inputs on
the options calculator,

140
00:06:56.750 --> 00:06:58.580
and click Calculate to better

141
00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:02.840
understand the impact of the
model impacts on the vega.

142
00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:04.640
Rho represents

143
00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:07.760
an option's price sensitivity
to interest rates.

144
00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:10.265
The greater the
underlying asset price

145
00:07:10.265 --> 00:07:12.470
and remaining dates
to exploration,

146
00:07:12.470 --> 00:07:14.525
the greater the rho.

147
00:07:14.525 --> 00:07:17.630
As an example, if the
interest rate were to

148
00:07:17.630 --> 00:07:20.645
rise from three and a half
to four and a half percent,

149
00:07:20.645 --> 00:07:22.340
average value of rho would

150
00:07:22.340 --> 00:07:25.470
approximate the change
in the option's price.

151
00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:28.720
Change the model inputs on
the options calculator,

152
00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:30.880
and click Calculate
to better understand

153
00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:34.590
the impact of the model
inputs on the Rho.

154
00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:37.670
Now, let's put it all together.

155
00:07:37.670 --> 00:07:39.965
Using the following assumptions,

156
00:07:39.965 --> 00:07:43.175
we can see the estimated
value of a call option.

157
00:07:43.175 --> 00:07:47.525
Let's use a share
price of a $35.90,

158
00:07:47.525 --> 00:07:50.045
a strike price of $40,

159
00:07:50.045 --> 00:07:53.510
and assume an interest
rate of four percent.

160
00:07:53.510 --> 00:07:56.630
Let's assume an implied
volatility reading of

161
00:07:56.630 --> 00:07:59.750
38.8 percent for the underlying,

162
00:07:59.750 --> 00:08:04.025
and say the option contract
has 38 days to expire.

163
00:08:04.025 --> 00:08:09.005
The call price works out
to be almost 54 cents.

164
00:08:09.005 --> 00:08:14.360
The measure return
for Delta is 0.2214.

165
00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:16.940
This means that for
every one dollar move

166
00:08:16.940 --> 00:08:18.950
in the underlying asset's price,

167
00:08:18.950 --> 00:08:23.165
the option value will change
by approximately 22 cents,

168
00:08:23.165 --> 00:08:25.445
ignoring the impact of Gamma.

169
00:08:25.445 --> 00:08:29.450
Alternatively, there is
a 22 percent chance that

170
00:08:29.450 --> 00:08:31.310
the option will expire at

171
00:08:31.310 --> 00:08:34.700
or above the strike price of $40.

172
00:08:34.700 --> 00:08:40.130
The Gamma reading of
0.0661 tells us that,

173
00:08:40.130 --> 00:08:43.040
should the underlying
price rise by one dollar,

174
00:08:43.040 --> 00:08:45.980
then Delta will rise
by approximately

175
00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:48.290
6.6 cents because of

176
00:08:48.290 --> 00:08:51.470
the curvature in the
options Priceline.

177
00:08:51.470 --> 00:08:58.295
The reading of minus 0.0184
for Theta tells us that

178
00:08:58.295 --> 00:09:00.470
the value of the
option contract drops

179
00:09:00.470 --> 00:09:05.495
approximately 1.8 cents
for each day that passes.

180
00:09:05.495 --> 00:09:10.760
The Vega of 0.0344
tells us that if

181
00:09:10.760 --> 00:09:13.730
implied volatility
were to increase from

182
00:09:13.730 --> 00:09:17.630
38.8 to 39.8 percent,

183
00:09:17.630 --> 00:09:20.090
then the value of the
option would increase

184
00:09:20.090 --> 00:09:23.690
by approximately 3.4 cents.

185
00:09:23.690 --> 00:09:28.700
The reading of 0.0077
for Rho tells us

186
00:09:28.700 --> 00:09:33.170
that if interest rates were
to increase from 4-5 percent,

187
00:09:33.170 --> 00:09:35.210
the option's value would increase

188
00:09:35.210 --> 00:09:39.260
by approximately 0.77 cents.

189
00:09:39.260 --> 00:09:42.110
Now that we have covered
the Greek risk factors,

190
00:09:42.110 --> 00:09:43.550
we encourage you to play with

191
00:09:43.550 --> 00:09:46.820
the free LIC options
calculator to understand how

192
00:09:46.820 --> 00:09:49.010
changes in all of the
inputs are likely to

193
00:09:49.010 --> 00:09:52.325
affect an option's
price and Greeks.

194
00:09:52.325 --> 00:09:53.975
It should be noted that

195
00:09:53.975 --> 00:09:56.330
inputs rarely change
in isolation,

196
00:09:56.330 --> 00:09:58.160
and so it is important to test

197
00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:01.530
the changing multiple
inputs at the same time.